Jump directly to the interaction by [clicking here](#clear-interaction).

As part of the Design Engineering program at the Harvard Graduate School of Design, I'm taking a class called Parts of Speech: Reimagining Computing Through Verbs, taught by [Jenny Rodenhouse](https://jennyrodenhouse.com/). I'll attach a brief excerpt from the syllabus here:

>Every interface is built from actions. We boot, click, swipe, friend, and cancel; each verb carries a history and a worldview about who and what computing is for. With large language models (LLMs) and text-to-form tools, a word can call a world into being in seconds, so choosing how to order a few letters is also a way of building one. This seminar takes verbs as its subject, studying how form and time direct a person to act.

Each week, we do a *verb study*. This involves:

1. Selecting a verb in response to a particular era of computing, researching its origins, etymology, and history through four lenses—illustrative, abstract, emotive and antithetical—and representing these through a type study.
2. Deconstructing each artifact through elements of design: line, color, shape, form, space and composition, texture.
3. Sketching the interaction at a low fidelity, before building an interactive version of it using code.

**Week 2:** 

We had to select a verb associated with the "Manipulate" era of computing, specifically in response to the verb "click". **I chose the verb "clear".** 

I liked "clear" since it has meanings both as a verb and an adjective. I also felt like it was interesting to think about how computing as a medium enabled "clearing" something away at the click of a button, similar to "undo". So much so, that the action of "clear" is often abstracted to be just an icon: <span class="inline-clear-icon" role="img" aria-label="an X icon"><XIcon size={16} /></span>.

Here are the type studies I did based on 4 different definitions of "clear":

<PhotoGallery items={typeStudies} label="Clear type studies" aspectRatio="3 / 2" fit="cover" />



I was really inspired by this Button Stealer Chrome extension (pictured below), which randomly "steals" buttons from websites as I browse the internet and creates an archive of sorts.

<PhotoGallery items={[{ src: buttonStealer, alt: 'An archive of buttons collected from websites, with different labels, colors and type treatments', caption: 'My Button Stealer collection' }]} label="Button Stealer reference" aspectRatio="1408 / 601" />

I connected this to the phenomenon of the many fake download buttons or "clear" ad buttons we have to contend with when on a "sketchier" part of the internet (such as on sports streaming sites). One wrong click, and you're screwed -- the interface probably gets populated with even more popups, a new tab showing an illicit website of some kind, or possibly a dangerous .exe in your Downloads folder. Browse with caution! None of this actually happens in the interaction below, so don't worry.


<Prototype id="clear-interaction" title="Clear: a grid of misleading buttons" document={clear} />

<p>FYI, there is a secret button each time that actually works to clear the screen. Reveal the spoiler to learn how! <span role="button" tabindex="0" class="spoiler" class:revealed={spoilerRevealed} aria-label={spoilerRevealed ? undefined : 'Reveal spoiler'} aria-expanded={spoilerRevealed} onclick={() => spoilerRevealed = !spoilerRevealed} onkeydown={(event) => { if (event.key === 'Enter' || event.key === ' ') { event.preventDefault(); spoilerRevealed = !spoilerRevealed; } }}><span aria-hidden={!spoilerRevealed}>The working button is the only one with the exact lowercase English word <strong>"clear"</strong>.</span></span></p>

I had fun demoing it in class and got good feedback! It could be interesting to abstract it a bit further, especially in the context of drawing or design applications, where 'clear' is a superpower that is most different from real life. This interaction ended up being closer to a piece of "net-art" than a pure interaction.

